{"slots": {"question": [{"claim": "The paper asks whether stigmatizing labels in Democratic Party campaign ads (labeling Trump as 'convicted criminal') spill over to worsen Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records broadly, and whether a counter-narrative using perspective-getting can mitigate these negative effects.", "quotes": [{"text": "this study explores what may have been the impact of propagating such labels on Democrats' perceptions of people with criminal records, and to what extent does the propagation of such stigmatizing terminology impact how well Democrats perceive to be represented by their own elected officials?", "page": 4}, {"text": "Additionally, this study seeks to understand whether providing a counterexample to Trump – highlighting the perspective of a formerly system-involved representative on the propagation of this label by the Democratic party – may counter-act potential negative impacts of the Party's Trump advertisement.", "page": 4}]}], "central_claim": [{"claim": "Exposure to the Democratic Party's Trump advertisement with the 'convicted criminal' label significantly worsened Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records, but a counter-narrative featuring a formerly system-involved state representative almost entirely ameliorated these negative effects.", "quotes": [{"text": "Results suggest that exposure to the Trump advertisement with the 'convicted criminal' label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats' perceptions of individuals with records, but improved perceived feelings of representation.", "page": 1}, {"text": "Findings suggest that the counter-stereotype vastly ameliorated the negative effects of the advertisement on Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records.", "page": 1}, {"text": "First, there is clear evidence that the Trump advertisement caused decreases in positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records amongst Democrats, but when paired with (and preceded by) a counter-narrative of a dedicated, system-impacted state representative who speaks to the harm of the 'criminal' label, these effects were almost entirely ameliorated", "page": 15}]}], "evidence": [{"claim": "The study is a survey experiment fielded via Prolific with 2,439 self-identified Democrats, randomly assigned to three treatment arms: a control group, a Trump ad alone group, and a counter-narrative first group.", "quotes": [{"text": "The 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the Control group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).", "page": 4}, {"text": "The survey was fielded through Prolific, an online survey platform that is commonly utilized for conducting survey-based research, which has been demonstrated as a reliable platform for obtaining high-quality data.", "page": 8}, {"text": "The primary outcomes of interest in this study were: a) perceptions of individuals with criminal records; b) perceived value of representation; c) perceived quality of Democratic representation in federal government; d) complexity of representation; e) motives of elected officials; and f) advertisement support.", "page": 7}]}], "key_findings": [{"claim": "Exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased Democrats' positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by approximately 0.83 standard deviation units.", "quotes": [{"text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records ( β̂0 = −0.835, SE = 0.044)", "page": 11}]}, {"claim": "The counter-narrative, when presented before the Trump ad, increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviation units relative to the ad alone, essentially offsetting the negative effect.", "quotes": [{"text": "exposure to the counter-narrative prior to being 