mp advertisement.", "page": 17}]}, {"claim": "It is not possible to disentangle the effect of the mugshot image from the stigmatizing terminology imposed on it.", "quotes": [{"text": "it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from the stigmatizing terminology imposed, and thus, this is recommended for exploration in future work.", "page": 17}]}, {"claim": "The durability of the effects and whether they would replicate in other contexts or less transparent designs remains unclear, as the study was a single time-point survey conducted after the election.", "quotes": [{"text": "the extent to which these results would replicate in other contexts and in less-transparent designs is unclear, as is the durability of effects.", "page": 17}, {"text": "the single time-point survey experimental approach is a useful first step, but leaves unanswered several key questions related to the ability of these results to replicate", "page": 17}]}, {"claim": "The study acknowledges concerns about potential generative AI contamination of online survey responses, though notes Prolific has comparatively low rates of AI infiltration.", "quotes": [{"text": "the extent to which these results would replicate in other contexts and in less-transparent designs is unclear, as is the durability of effects.", "page": 17}, {"text": "While this survey was completed before the introduction of Prolific's bot detection – which is actively deployed in a beta version at the time this paper was submitted – Prolific has also been documented as having comparatively one of the lowest percentages of bots infiltrating their survey base", "page": 17}]}], "theoretical_positioning": [{"claim": "The authors extend social categorization theory to the specific domain of political messaging about criminal records, applying its concepts of category salience and group exemplars to predict spillover from Trump's labeled status to broader perceptions of individuals with records.", "quotes": [{"text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such labels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents' perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.", "page": 1}, {"text": "once the label of 'felon' and 'convicted criminal' was applied to Donald J. Trump – and done so quite routinely and saliently – theory and evidence would predict greater homogenization and spillover perceptions from Trump, such that perceptions of individuals with records would be worsened amongst Democrats after exposure to the advertisement", "page": 2}]}, {"claim": "The authors combine social categorization theory with the perspective-getting literature, positioning their counter-narrative intervention as an application of established perspective-getting findings to the domain of political representation and criminal justice.", "quotes": [{"text": "the strength of the counter-narrative is not unexpected, but promising, given prior evidence on the effects of perspective-getting and counter-stereotypical narratives on increasing empathy for members of an outgroup", "page": 15}, {"text": "Perspective-getting, or being provided with the perspective of another, has been shown to significantly increase feelings of empathy for members of an out-group [45,46], including incarcerated individuals [47].", "page": 3}]}], "interlocutor_claim": [{"claim": "The paper attributes to the affective polarization and emotional campaigning literature (Abramowitz & Webster; Kushner Gadarian & Brader; Versteegen) the claim that invoking emotional responses encourages political support by heightening in-group ties, and then disputes this by citing evidence that policy alignment is crucial to feelings of representation.", "quotes": [{"text": "a considerable body of evidence suggests that invoking an emotional response may encourage political support, which may downplay focusing on representation regarding policy positions, leaning instead into heightening in-group ties and fostering affective polarization [30–32]", "page": 3}, {"text": "However, this is not necessarily concrete, in that alignment of policy standpoints has been shown to be crucial to feelings of representation [34–36].", "page": 3}]}]}}
